1.28.0: Super 8 that looks like Super 8

The Super 8 still was nearly indistinguishable from its high-resolution
source: softness was a one-pixel blur whatever the input, and the
canonical stock was the most saturated slide film in the library.

- format resolution: a preset can state what its format resolves, in
  cycles across the frame at MTF50, applied as a gaussian of that
  width. Super 8: 145 cycles (about 25 lp/mm on a 5.8 mm frame). A
  24 MP still and a 720-line movie frame now soften alike.
- grain sized to the frame for such a format (3.5 px on a 6000-wide
  frame), so stills carry the coarse grain movies always had.
- projection stage after the film, LUT or scalar: saturation 0.74,
  blacks lifted 3.5%, a little warmth, contrast 0.92.
- scalar tone calmed (contrast 1.08, saturation 0.96); canonical stock
  kodak_kodachrome_64 instead of kodak_ektachrome_100_vs.
- other cameras are untouched (their renders are byte-identical).

Tests: stripes at 60 and 300 cycles per frame at two image sizes (the
coarse kept, the fine gone, at both); a saturated red comes out no
more saturated than it went in and far less than off a 35mm slide; the
Super 8 reference image regenerated.
Docs: PRESET_SOURCES.md.
